speechtext:
I say it through you, Mr. Speaker, to my hon. friends opposite. If they are opposed to that increase in the public expenditure, made up as I have stated, then they are perfectly justified in criticising; but if they are not opposed to these items of public expenditure, is it fair to send the charge throughout the country that the Government has been reckless in its expenditure of public money and in increasing the expenditure last year by $100,000,000?
The next item my hon. friend takes exception to is a much smaller one-but sometimes (you know) you can awaken more interest in the public mind in a small item than in a large one. I am; therefore, going to take up one or two small items, particularly as some reference was made to them this afternoon by the leader of the Opposition. The hon. member for Antigonish and Guysborough referred to an item of $200,000 as being the expenditure of the Department of Public Information. He stated that this department was organized for political purposes and for no other purpose, and that;
Whatever value It may have had as a political agency to assist the Union Government, he would be a bold man who would say that it was worth two hundred cents to the people of Canada.

